- Optionally remove `live_output_callback` from the `pub use spinner::...` re-export list (line 40)

#### Step 7: Import for `OutputLine` in `SpinnerHandle`

The `create_output_callback` method signature references `OutputLine`. This type is from `crate::shell::OutputLine`. The `SpinnerHandle` trait in `ui/mod.rs` will need to import it. Check if this creates any circular dependency -- it should not, since `shell` is independent from `ui`.

### Handling the `&self` vs `&mut self` Problem

The `UserInterface` trait methods all take `&mut self`. The new `format_step_header` and `format_status` methods are pure formatters that do not mutate state. However, in the runner, `ui` is `&mut dyn UserInterface`, so calling `&self` methods on it works fine (a `&mut` reference can be reborrowed as `&`). The key constraint is that the runner cannot hold a `&self` borrow of `ui` at the same time as a `&mut self` borrow.

Looking at the code, `step_display` (the formatted header) is stored as a `String` before any `&mut` calls. This is fine -- `format_step_header` returns an owned `String`, so no borrow is held. Similarly, `format_status` returns an owned `String`.

There is no problem here.

### Impact on Existing Tests

The existing workflow tests in `workflow.rs` use `MockUI` which captures messages as strings. The tests check for:
- `result.success`
- `result.steps.len()`
- `ui.prompts_shown().contains(...)`
- `marker.exists()`

None of them check the exact styled formatting of step headers or skip messages. The `MockUI` captures whatever string is passed to `ui.message(...)`. After the refactor, the strings will be produced by `ui.format_step_header(...)` (which uses the default plain implementation on `MockUI`), so they will look slightly different from the current styled output (no ANSI codes since `BivvyTheme::new()` was producing ANSI in tests). But since tests use `.contains(...)` checks (e.g., `ui.has_message("skipped")`), and the plain defaults preserve the text content, all tests should continue to pass.

The one subtle risk: tests that check for exact message strings with ANSI escape codes would break. A grep for test assertions on theme-formatted strings would verify this. Based on the test code I've read, no tests assert on exact ANSI-styled strings in the runner module.

